/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Explorers and Everyday Inventions */
WB.L("longitude-problem", "explore", "advanced", "The longitude problem: how a clock saved sailors",
  "For centuries, ships could find how far north or south they were, but not how far east or west. Solving it took a prize from Parliament and a self-taught clockmaker.",
  [
    ["Lost in the fog", "Without a way to find longitude, even skilled navigators could be badly wrong about where they were.",
      "On a foggy night in October 1707, a British fleet returning from the Mediterranean ran onto the rocks of the Isles of Scilly, off Cornwall. Four warships sank and well over a thousand sailors drowned, including Admiral Sir Cloudesley Shovell. Historians still debate exactly what went wrong, but the disaster became a symbol of a problem every sailor knew.\n\nOut of sight of land, crews worked out their position by estimating speed, heading and time elapsed, a method called dead reckoning. Small errors piled up day after day. A captain could believe he was safely at sea while his ship headed straight for a coastline he could not see."],
    ["Longitude is a time problem", "Because the Earth turns 15 degrees every hour, knowing the time at home reveals how far east or west you are.",
      "Finding latitude, your position north or south, was fairly simple. Sailors measured the Sun's height at noon, or the Pole Star at night, and used tables to turn the angle into a position.\n\nLongitude, your position east or west, was far harder because the Earth spins. It turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, which is 15 degrees every hour. So if you know the time at home and the local time where you are, the difference tells you how far east or west you have travelled.\n\nLocal noon is easy to spot from the Sun. The hard part was knowing the time back home, because the clocks of the day could not keep accurate time on a rolling, damp ship."],
    ["Prize and astronomers", "Britain offered a fortune for a solution in 1714, and astronomers first answered by using the Moon as a clock.",
      "Trade and naval power depended on safe navigation, so in 1714 Parliament passed the Longitude Act. It offered up to 20,000 pounds, a fortune then, for a method that found longitude to within half a degree, with smaller rewards for less accurate ones. A panel later called the Board of Longitude judged entries, and \"finding the longitude\" became a joke for chasing the impossible.\n\nMost experts expected astronomy to answer. The Moon moves steadily against the stars, so a navigator could measure the angle between the Moon and a bright star, look it up in tables, and find the time at Greenwich. It worked, but needed clear skies, a precise instrument and hours of calculation. Nevil Maskelyne's Nautical Almanac, first published in 1766, made it far more usable."],
    ["Harrison's sea clocks", "A self-taught carpenter, John Harrison, built clocks accurate enough to solve the problem, but the Board was slow to reward him.",
      "John Harrison was a carpenter from the north of England with no formal clockmaking training. From the 1730s he built large sea clocks, H1 to H3, designed to keep time despite a ship's motion and temperature changes. His fourth, H4, finished in 1759, looked like an oversized pocket watch, about 13 centimetres across. On a trial voyage to Jamaica in 1761 and 1762 it lost only a few seconds over weeks at sea, far better than the Act required.\n\nYet the Board demanded more trials and insisted he explain his design so others could copy it. Some historians call this reasonable caution; others see bias towards the astronomers. In 1773, after appealing to King George III, Harrison received a substantial sum from Parliament, though never officially the prize. He died in 1776."],
    ["Chronometers to GPS", "Accurate clocks became standard at sea, and precise time still underpins navigation today.",
      "Watchmaker Larcum Kendall made a copy of H4, known as K1, and James Cook carried it on his second Pacific voyage in the 1770s. Cook praised its accuracy. Over the following decades, marine chronometers became cheaper and more reliable, and by the 1800s they were standard on naval and merchant ships. Harrison's clocks survive and are displayed at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich.\n\nIn 1884 an international conference chose the meridian through Greenwich as the world's prime meridian, longitude zero.\n\nThe idea behind Harrison's work has never gone away. Satellite navigation works by measuring tiny differences in the time signals sent from satellites carrying atomic clocks. Your phone finds where you are, in a sense, by solving the same time puzzle that once drowned sailors."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why could navigators be badly wrong about their position at sea?", ["Compasses stopped working whenever there was fog","Small errors in dead reckoning built up daily","Latitude could not be measured out at sea","Coastal charts were usually missing entirely"], 1, "Estimating speed, heading and time let small errors accumulate until a ship could be far from where its crew believed."],
    [1, "Why does knowing the time at home help you find longitude?", ["Clocks keep slower time near the equator","Tides change at fixed times along each line of longitude","The Earth turns 15 degrees each hour","The Sun climbs higher at noon in the east"], 2, "A time difference converts directly to distance east or west because the Earth rotates at a steady rate."],
    [2, "How did the lunar distance method work as a clock?", ["It timed the tides against the Moon","It matched the Sun's colour at sunset to a table","It tracked the ship's compass bearing","It measured the Moon's angle from a star"], 3, "The Moon's steady motion against the stars acts as a clock in the sky, once you can measure and look it up."],
    [3, "What was remarkable about Harrison's H4?", ["It was watch-sized and kept time at sea","It was powered by the motion of waves","It was a huge pendulum clock on deck","It needed an astronomer to operate it"], 0, "H4 lost only a few seconds over weeks at sea, proving a mechanical timekeeper could solve longitude."],
    [4, "How does satellite navigation echo Harrison's idea?", ["It uses compasses in orbit","It measures the angle of the Moon","It compares time signals from atomic clocks","It reads star charts stored in phones"], 2, "Both solve position by comparing precise times, so accurate clocks still underpin navigation."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Sailors could find north and south easily, but east and west drowned thousands. Why?"});

WB.L("accidental-inventions", "explore", "beginner", "Happy accidents: inventions nobody planned",
  "Some of the most useful things in your home began as mistakes, spills and puzzling surprises. The trick was having someone there who noticed.",
  [
    ["The prepared mind", "Lucky accidents become inventions only when someone with knowledge notices the surprise and asks why.",
      "Louis Pasteur is said to have remarked that chance favours the prepared mind. Invention is full of examples: a surprising result turns up, most people wipe it away, and someone with the right knowledge stops and asks why.\n\nAccidental discovery is sometimes called serendipity, a word coined in 1754 by the English writer Horace Walpole, inspired by a fairy tale about three princes of Serendip, an old name for Sri Lanka, who kept making lucky finds.\n\nThese stories have been retold many times and sometimes polished. Each still shows that a lucky moment needed skill and persistence to become useful."],
    ["Rubber and X-rays", "Goodyear's stove and Röntgen's glowing screen led to vulcanised rubber and X-rays because both men investigated the surprise.",
      "Natural rubber was frustrating: sticky and smelly in summer, cracked in winter. American inventor Charles Goodyear experimented for years while sinking into debt. By his own account, around 1839 he let a rubber and sulphur mixture touch a hot stove. It charred like leather but stayed flexible. This was vulcanisation, named after Vulcan, the Roman god of fire. Heating rubber with sulphur links its long molecules together, making tyres and seals possible. Goodyear died in debt.\n\nIn November 1895, German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen noticed a coated screen glowing near a cathode ray tube he had covered in black cardboard. He called the unknown rays X. They passed through flesh but not bone, and one early image showed his wife's hand with her ring. He won the first Nobel Prize in Physics in 1901."],
    ["Microwaves and Velcro", "A melted chocolate bar and a walk with burrs led to the microwave oven and Velcro.",
      "Raytheon built magnetrons, devices that make microwaves for radar. The story goes that engineer Percy Spencer noticed a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted near one, then aimed the beam at popcorn kernels, which popped. Microwaves make water molecules in food jiggle, and that motion becomes heat. Raytheon patented the idea in 1945. Its first oven, the Radarange, was fridge-sized and expensive. Affordable home models came in the late 1960s and 1970s.\n\nIn 1941, Swiss engineer George de Mestral came back from an Alpine walk with burrs clinging to his trousers and dog. Under a microscope he saw tiny hooks catching loops of thread. A strip of hooks and a strip of loops could make a fastener. After years of work he patented Velcro, from velours (velvet) and crochet (hook). Copying nature this way is called biomimicry."],
    ["A glue that didn't stick", "A failed super-strong adhesive became Post-it Notes once a colleague found a problem it could solve.",
      "In 1968, Spencer Silver, a chemist at the American company 3M, was trying to make a very strong adhesive. Instead he made a weak one. It stuck to surfaces but peeled off cleanly and could be reused. Silver found it interesting and spent years promoting it inside the company, but nobody could think of a product.\n\nThe answer came from his colleague Art Fry, who sang in a church choir and was annoyed that his paper bookmarks kept falling out of his hymn book. He realised Silver's glue could make a bookmark that stayed put without damaging the pages.\n\nAfter testing and false starts, 3M launched Post-it Notes nationally in the United States in 1980. A solution had waited years for the right problem."],
    ["Luck needs hard work", "A lucky moment is only the start; turning it into something useful takes years of effort.",
      "It is tempting to think great inventions arrive in a flash, but every story here involved long, unglamorous work. Goodyear experimented for years before his stove moment. Röntgen spent weeks testing his rays before announcing anything. De Mestral took more than a decade to turn burrs into a product, and the Post-it took roughly twelve years from odd glue to shop shelves.\n\nEach discovery also depended on someone who understood enough to see that a surprise mattered. Modern research organisations try to leave room for this by giving scientists time to follow curious results.\n\nThe lesson works in everyday life too. When something goes unexpectedly, ask what it might be telling you."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do accidents lead to inventions only for some people?", ["Accidents happen far more often to inventors","Most accidents are secretly planned in advance","Someone with the right knowledge asks why","Only well-funded laboratories have accidents"], 2, "Chance favours the prepared mind: the knowledge to recognise a surprise turns luck into discovery."],
    [1, "What does heating rubber with sulphur do?", ["Dissolves the rubber into a thick liquid","Links its long molecules so it stays flexible","Makes it sticky and smelly in summer","Turns it permanently rigid and brittle"], 1, "Vulcanisation cross-links rubber's molecules so it stays flexible without melting or cracking."],
    [2, "Why did the microwave idea work on food?", ["Microwaves freeze water in food, which then melts","Microwaves jiggle water molecules, creating heat","The radar tube warmed food with direct flame","Microwaves heat only the surface of metal"], 1, "Microwaves energise water molecules, which is why watery food heats quickly."],
    [3, "Why did Silver's weak glue become useful?", ["It was later strengthened with sulphur","It proved stronger than anything else sold","Fry used it for bookmarks that stayed put","It dissolved in water so was easy to remove"], 2, "Its weakness was the strength: it stuck, then peeled off cleanly and could be reused."],
    [4, "What do these inventions have in common beyond luck?", ["Each was turned into a product within days","Each took years of effort to become useful","Each was developed by a large company team","Each was patented before the accident happened"], 1, "A lucky moment is only the start; the hard work and understanding come after."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A melted chocolate bar, a sticky burr, a failed glue. Which one changed your kitchen?"});

WB.L("refrigeration", "explore", "intermediate", "Keeping cool: the story of refrigeration",
  "Your fridge is so ordinary you barely notice it. Yet keeping things cold changed what we eat, how we shop and even how Australia earned its living.",
  [
    ["Ice from a frozen pond", "Before machines, cold was a luxury cut from nature, stored in pits and shipped surprising distances.",
      "In 1806, Boston businessman Frederic Tudor loaded a ship with ice cut from frozen New England ponds and sent it to the Caribbean island of Martinique. People laughed, and much of it melted, but Tudor kept going. He improved insulation using sawdust and built a huge trade, eventually shipping ice as far as India. He became known as the Ice King.\n\nLong before Tudor, people in many cultures stored winter ice and snow in pits, cellars and ice houses packed with straw. In ancient Persia, domed structures called yakhchals held ice through hot summers.\n\nCold was gathered from nature and guarded carefully."],
    ["The science of cold", "A fridge doesn't make cold; it moves heat out of the box by evaporating and compressing a fluid.",
      "A fridge does not make cold. It moves heat from inside the box to the outside. The key is an effect you feel when you step out of a swimming pool: as liquid evaporates, it absorbs heat and cools its surroundings.\n\nIn 1748 Scottish professor William Cullen showed that evaporating ether in a partial vacuum could freeze a little water, though he built no practical machine.\n\nThe trick is to evaporate a liquid inside the cold area, then pump the vapour away and compress it outside, where it releases its heat and turns back into liquid. Run that cycle continuously and heat keeps flowing out. That is why the back of a fridge feels warm."],
    ["Geelong and frozen meat", "Australian refrigeration pioneers and a surplus of meat paved the way for the first frozen meat shipment to London in 1880.",
      "One pioneer of mechanical refrigeration was James Harrison, a Scottish-born printer and journalist who ran the Geelong Advertiser in Victoria. A popular story says ether used to clean printing type made the metal cold as it evaporated. By the 1850s he had built an ice machine using compressed ether vapour, patented in the middle of that decade. Breweries, needing cold for lager-style beer, and meat works used it. His bigger dream, shipping frozen meat to Britain, failed in 1873 and left him broke.\n\nAustralia had huge numbers of sheep and cattle but few people to eat them, while British cities wanted cheap meat. In early 1880 the steamship Strathleven reached London with frozen beef and mutton loaded in Sydney and Melbourne, in good condition. Frozen meat, then dairy and fruit, became major exports."],
    ["The home fridge arrives", "Early home fridges used dangerous gases, and the replacement CFCs later turned out to damage the ozone layer.",
      "For decades many households used an icebox, an insulated cabinet cooled by a block of ice delivered by the iceman. Electric home refrigerators appeared in the early twentieth century, but they were expensive, and early models used toxic or flammable gases such as ammonia, sulphur dioxide and methyl chloride. Leaks could be deadly.\n\nAround 1930, American chemist Thomas Midgley helped develop chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, which seemed perfectly safe. Decades later scientists found CFCs were destroying the ozone layer that shields Earth from harmful ultraviolet light. The 1987 Montreal Protocol phased them out, and it is widely seen as one of the most successful environmental agreements ever.\n\nReplacement gases have been changed again since, because some trap heat in the atmosphere."],
    ["Cold in daily life", "Cold underpins modern food and medicine, so keep your fridge at 5°C or below and mind its energy use.",
      "Before fridges, many people shopped for perishable food almost daily and preserved it by salting, smoking, drying, pickling or canning. Refrigeration let families shop weekly and made supermarkets possible. A cold chain now links farms, ships, trucks and shops, so fresh produce travels far and is eaten out of season. Many vaccines and medicines also need narrow temperature ranges.\n\nAt home, Australian food safety authorities recommend keeping your fridge at 5°C or below, because most bacteria that cause food poisoning grow slowly at that temperature. A cheap thermometer checks it. Fridges run all day, so compare the Energy Rating label when buying. Worn door seals, an oversized fridge or no room behind it all raise power use, and an old second fridge in the garage can quietly add to your bill."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did people keep ice through hot summers before machines?", ["They stored winter ice in pits and ice houses","They made it on demand with chemical reactions","They shipped it daily in from the tropics","They buried it deep in the desert sand"], 0, "Cold was a luxury gathered from nature and stored in insulated pits, cellars and ice houses."],
    [1, "Why does the back of a fridge feel warm?", ["The light bulb heats it","The motor is faulty","Warm air leaks in through the door","Heat taken from inside is released there"], 3, "A fridge moves heat from inside to outside rather than creating cold."],
    [2, "Why did refrigeration link Australia and Britain so effectively?", ["Australia had surplus meat and Britain wanted cheap meat","Britain had vast surplus farmland to spare","Australian ships were faster than British ones","Frozen meat was cheaper to produce in Britain"], 0, "Refrigerated ships connected a country with surplus meat to a hungry overseas market."],
    [3, "What problem did scientists later find with CFCs?", ["They exploded easily","They poisoned stored food","They damaged the ozone layer","They froze too slowly"], 2, "Gases that seemed safe for people were found to damage the ozone layer, leading to the Montreal Protocol."],
    [4, "Why do authorities recommend keeping a fridge at 5°C or below?", ["It stops every kind of bacteria growing completely","Most food-poisoning bacteria grow slowly at that temperature","It uses the least possible amount of electricity","It keeps all food at exactly the temperature of ice"], 1, "Cold slows the growth of food-poisoning bacteria, so a fridge thermometer helps keep food safe."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"In 1880 a steamship carried frozen Australian meat to London. How did it stay cold?"});

WB.L("toilets-sewers", "explore", "beginner", "Flushing toilets and the Great Stink",
  "Few inventions have saved as many lives as the humble toilet and the sewer pipe behind it. Their story involves a smelly river, a deadly pump and a lot of bricks.",
  [
    ["The summer London stank", "The Great Stink of 1858 pushed politicians to build London's sewers once they could smell the problem themselves.",
      "The summer of 1858 in London was unusually hot, and the River Thames was full of human waste. The smell was so overpowering that it was nicknamed the Great Stink. Beside the river, curtains in the Houses of Parliament were reportedly soaked in chemicals to mask the odour, and politicians considered moving elsewhere.\n\nFor years engineers had warned that London's drains were failing, but little had happened. Now the people in charge could smell the problem for themselves. Within weeks Parliament passed a law giving the Metropolitan Board of Works the power and money to build a proper sewer system. It became one of the great engineering projects of the Victorian age."],
    ["Before the flush", "Most of history's waste went into pits and streets, and the belief that bad air caused disease hid the real danger.",
      "Some ancient cities managed waste well. Indus Valley homes had drains linked to covered channels, and Rome had large sewers, including the Cloaca Maxima, which still exists. In many later towns, people emptied chamber pots into cesspits, the street or a river, or paid nightsoil men to cart it away. As cities grew, cesspits overflowed into wells, and drinking-water rivers carried sewage. Few saw the danger: the dominant theory blamed disease on bad air, or miasma.\n\nThe flush toilet arrived slowly. Around 1596 the Elizabethan courtier Sir John Harington described one with a tank and valve, but it did not catch on. In 1775 Scottish watchmaker Alexander Cumming patented an S-bend: water held in the bend seals out smelly gases, an idea still used today. Thomas Crapper, a real Victorian plumber, did not invent it."],
    ["John Snow's pump", "John Snow mapped the 1854 cholera outbreak and showed that contaminated water, not bad air, spread the disease.",
      "Cholera struck London several times in the 1800s, killing tens of thousands. In 1854 a terrible outbreak hit Soho. Doctor John Snow doubted the miasma theory and suspected cholera spread through water. He mapped where victims lived and found they clustered around a public pump on Broad Street.\n\nHe persuaded local officials to remove the pump's handle, and the outbreak, already declining, ended. Later investigation suggested the well had been contaminated by a nearby cesspit.\n\nSnow's map became a classic of public health and epidemiology. His ideas weren't widely accepted at the time, but they helped show that clean water and safe sewage disposal were matters of life and death."],
    ["Bazalgette's sewers", "Joseph Bazalgette built London's sewer network large enough for future growth, and parts of it still work today.",
      "The engineer given the job of fixing London was Joseph Bazalgette. From 1859 his teams built a vast network of brick sewers, with large intercepting sewers running beside the Thames to carry waste east, away from the centre, and release it further downstream. The system included around 1,800 kilometres of street sewers.\n\nBazalgette is often praised for his foresight: when calculating the pipe sizes he needed, he reportedly doubled them to allow for the city's growth. Parts of the network still work today. The project also created the Victoria Embankment and other riverside roads.\n\nCholera outbreaks in the city declined, and his work is credited with saving countless lives."],
    ["Smellbourne and beyond", "Australian cities faced the same stink, and safe sanitation, still missing for billions, remains a great health advance.",
      "Australia's booming cities were no better. In the 1880s Melbourne was among the richest cities in the world, but waste drained into open gutters, cesspits and the Yarra. Critics punned on \"Marvellous Melbourne\" and called it Marvellous Smellbourne. Typhoid was a constant threat. A board of works formed in 1891, and later that decade the first homes were connected to a sewer, with waste pumped to a treatment farm at Werribee. Sydney built sewers through the late 1800s. Around 1980 Australian company Caroma developed the dual flush toilet, saving large amounts of water.\n\nIn 2007, British Medical Journal readers voted sanitation the greatest medical milestone since 1840, ahead of antibiotics and vaccines. By UN estimates, roughly 3.5 billion people still lack safely managed sanitation. World Toilet Day is 19 November."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Parliament act on London's sewage in 1858?", ["Engineers finally produced a cheap new design","The stench reached the politicians themselves","Cholera deaths in the city had suddenly stopped","A foreign visitor complained about the river"], 1, "The problem had long been known; the smell made it impossible to ignore."],
    [1, "What does the S-bend in a toilet do?", ["Makes toilets flush with less water","Lets toilets be built upstairs","Holds water that blocks smells","Makes the flush much faster"], 2, "The water sitting in the bend forms a seal against gases from the sewer."],
    [2, "What did John Snow's map show about cholera?", ["Victims clustered around one water pump","The disease was spread by bad air","Victims were all from one family","Cholera was caused by cold weather"], 0, "By mapping cases, Snow showed that contaminated water, not miasma, spread the disease."],
    [3, "Why is Bazalgette praised for his sewers?", ["He invented the flush toilet","He discovered how cholera spreads","He made pipes larger to allow for growth","He moved Parliament away from the river"], 2, "Building in spare capacity let the network serve a growing city and some of it still works today."],
    [4, "What does the dual flush toilet help Australians do?", ["Avoid needing sewers","Save large amounts of water","Treat sewage at home","Prevent cholera"], 1, "Two flush options let people use less water for liquid waste."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A smelly river forced Parliament's hand in 1858. What changed after the stink?"});

WB.L("burke-and-wills", "explore", "intermediate", "Burke and Wills: the expedition that went wrong",
  "In 1860 a lavishly equipped expedition left Melbourne cheered by thousands. Within a year its leaders were dead, starving beside a creek where others had lived for millennia.",
  [
    ["A grand departure", "The lavishly equipped Victorian Exploring Expedition of 1860 set out to cross Australia from south to north, led by a man with no exploring experience.",
      "On 20 August 1860, crowds gathered in Royal Park, Melbourne, to farewell the Victorian Exploring Expedition. Its goal was to cross the continent to the Gulf of Carpentaria, over 3,000 kilometres through country few colonists had seen. Organised by the Royal Society of Victoria, it was one of the best equipped expeditions in Australian history, with around nineteen men, horses, wagons and about two dozen camels imported from India. Victoria, rich from the gold rushes, wanted the glory of being first, and perhaps a route for a telegraph line.\n\nIts leader, Robert O'Hara Burke, was an Irish-born police officer known for courage and charm, but with no experience of exploration or the inland. William John Wills, a young surveyor, handled navigation."],
    ["Trouble from the start", "An overloaded party, quarrels and fear of a rival led Burke to split his men and dash north.",
      "Problems began almost immediately. The expedition was overloaded and slow, and quarrels broke out. At Menindee, on the Darling River, the second in command, George Landells, resigned after clashing with Burke. Burke feared a rival South Australian expedition led by John McDouall Stuart might beat him, so he grew impatient and began splitting his party to move faster.\n\nHe set up a depot at Cooper Creek, in what is now south-west Queensland. In December 1860, with summer heat building, he set out for the Gulf with just three companions, Wills, Charles Gray and John King, plus a horse and six camels. He left William Brahe in charge of the depot, to wait about three months, or longer if possible."],
    ["The Gulf, then the return", "The party reached the northern coast but starved on the way back, with every delay costing them.",
      "The small party moved north quickly. By around February 1861 they reached the tidal channels and mangroves near the Flinders River. Swamps blocked their view of the open sea, but they had effectively crossed the continent.\n\nThe return was a grim march. Food ran low, and the wet season turned the ground to mud. The men killed and ate several camels and their horse. Charles Gray grew ill and weak, and Burke at first suspected him of pretending. When Gray was caught taking extra flour, Burke reportedly hit him. Gray died in April 1861, and burying him cost a day.\n\nEvery delay mattered, because they relied on Brahe still being at the depot. Brahe's men had already waited longer than three months, and some were sick with scurvy."],
    ["Missing by hours", "The survivors reached Cooper Creek on the very day the depot party left, and could not catch up.",
      "On the evening of 21 April 1861, Burke, Wills and King staggered back into the Cooper Creek depot. It was empty. Brahe's party had left that same morning, only hours earlier.\n\nOn a coolibah tree, Brahe's men had carved a message telling them to dig. Buried beneath were supplies and a note. The tree, now known as the Dig Tree, still stands beside the creek.\n\nToo weak to catch up with Brahe, Burke decided to head for Mount Hopeless, the nearest South Australian pastoral outpost, rather than follow the known route home. In a cruel twist, Brahe returned briefly to the depot later, but did not notice signs that Burke had been there, and left again."],
    ["Survival and legacy", "The Yandruwandha kept John King alive, and the disaster became a lesson in ignoring local knowledge.",
      "The three men never reached Mount Hopeless. The Yandruwandha people, who had lived there for thousands of years, gave them fish and cakes made from nardoo, a fern whose spore cases are ground into flour. Burke at one point fired a pistol to drive them away. Historians think the explorers skipped the traditional preparation, which removes a substance that destroys vitamin B1, worsening their starvation. Burke and Wills died around the end of June 1861. King survived with the Yandruwandha until Alfred Howitt's rescue party found him in September.\n\nA Royal Commission criticised Burke's judgement and the organisers. In 1863 the pair received a grand public funeral in Melbourne. Stuart's party crossed south to north and returned in 1862. Today the story is a lesson in poor leadership and in ignoring Aboriginal knowledge."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was a major weakness of Robert O'Hara Burke as expedition leader?", ["He refused to use camels","He was too cautious to continue","He had no experience of exploring","He could not ride a horse"], 2, "Courage and charm could not replace the skills needed to lead an expedition through unknown country."],
    [1, "Why did Burke split his party and rush north?", ["Supplies had run out at Menindee","He feared a rival would cross first","The government ordered him to hurry","He wanted to avoid the Yandruwandha"], 1, "Worry about being beaten by John McDouall Stuart made Burke impatient and led to risky decisions."],
    [2, "Why was every delay on the return journey so dangerous?", ["The camels could not travel in the wet","They relied on supplies at the depot","The Gulf had no fresh water","Their maps were wrong"], 1, "With food low, survival depended on reaching Cooper Creek before Brahe's party left."],
    [3, "What happened when the survivors reached Cooper Creek?", ["The depot was abandoned only hours earlier","They found Brahe waiting with fresh supplies","Floods had destroyed the depot","A rescue party had just arrived"], 0, "Missing Brahe by hours turned a rescue into a fatal trap."],
    [4, "What kept John King alive after Burke and Wills died?", ["A police patrol from South Australia","Pastoralists from a nearby station","Care and food from the Yandruwandha people","Supplies buried at the Dig Tree"], 2, "Aboriginal knowledge of the country kept King alive where the explorers starved."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"They crossed a continent and missed rescue by hours. What went wrong at Cooper Creek?"});

WB.L("ibn-battuta", "explore", "intermediate", "Ibn Battuta: the greatest traveller of the Middle Ages",
  "In 1325 a young Moroccan set off on a pilgrimage to Mecca. He came home almost a quarter of a century later, having seen more of the world than anyone of his age.",
  [
    ["Leaving Tangier", "A 21-year-old set out for Mecca in 1325 and did not come home for around 24 years.",
      "In June 1325, 21-year-old Ibn Battuta left his home in Tangier, in what is now Morocco, to make the hajj, the pilgrimage to Mecca that Muslims aim to complete at least once. He came from a family of legal scholars and was trained in Islamic law. He set out alone, and later described weeping at leaving his parents. He expected the journey to take a year or two.\n\nInstead he would not return for around 24 years. His travels took him across North Africa, the Middle East, East Africa, Central Asia, India, South-East Asia and, by his account, China, a distance often estimated at well over 100,000 kilometres."],
    ["A world connected", "A shared faith, language and legal tradition let a scholar find welcome and work far from home.",
      "How could a young man travel so far in the 1300s? Much of the answer lies in his world. Islam had spread from Spain and West Africa to India and parts of South-East Asia. Across it, educated Muslims shared Arabic as a language of learning, followed the same broad body of Islamic law and recognised each other's scholarship.\n\nA trained legal scholar like Ibn Battuta could arrive in a distant city and be welcomed by rulers and religious leaders, who often gave travellers gifts, lodging and sometimes jobs. Pilgrim caravans, trade routes and religious lodges supported him along the way.\n\nIt was a network as much as a map."],
    ["Mecca, Africa and Delhi", "He explored the Middle East and East African ports, then served as a judge for the Sultan of Delhi.",
      "Ibn Battuta reached Mecca in 1326 but kept exploring, travelling through Iraq and Persia. Around 1330 he sailed down the Red Sea and East African coast, visiting trading cities such as Mogadishu, Mombasa and Kilwa, which traded gold and ivory across the Indian Ocean. He later crossed Anatolia and the steppes of Central Asia, joining a Mongol khan's court.\n\nNext he sought India, where the Sultan of Delhi, Muhammad bin Tughluq, was famous for generosity to foreign scholars. In the 1330s he was appointed a qadi, or judge, on a large salary, and stayed about eight years. But the Sultan was also notorious for sudden cruelty, and Ibn Battuta feared for his life at one point. Chosen to lead an embassy to China, he saw his party attacked and the gift ships wrecked."],
    ["Islands and China", "After losing the embassy he worked in the Maldives and, by his own account, reached China, though historians doubt parts of that account.",
      "Having lost the embassy, and afraid to return to Delhi, Ibn Battuta went to the Maldives. He served as a judge there for around a year, married several times and tried, with mixed success, to impose stricter religious law. He visited Sri Lanka, climbing the sacred mountain Adam's Peak, then continued to Bengal and South-East Asia.\n\nBy his account he reached China in the 1340s, describing its ports, paper money and porcelain. Some historians doubt how far into China he really went, because parts of this section are vague or seem borrowed from other sources. Even so, his descriptions show a world joined by busy sea trade."],
    ["Mali and the Rihla", "His travels survive in the Rihla, a book that historians treat with care.",
      "Ibn Battuta returned to Morocco in 1349 and learned that his mother had died of the plague. Soon he was travelling again: to Granada, then in the early 1350s across the Sahara by camel caravan to the Mali Empire, famous for its gold. He visited the ruler, Mansa Sulayman, and Timbuktu. He admired the safe travel, but disliked some customs, revealing his biases.\n\nThe Sultan of Fez asked him to record his journeys. He dictated to a scholar, Ibn Juzayy, and the book, the Rihla (journey), was completed around 1355. Historians read it carefully: it was written from memory years later, some passages seem copied from earlier writers, and a few journeys may be exaggerated. Yet it is one of the richest records of the fourteenth century, and a lunar crater bears his name."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Ibn Battuta leave Tangier in 1325?", ["To trade spices","To study astronomy in China","To escape a war","To make the pilgrimage to Mecca"], 3, "He set out for the hajj, expecting a year or two, and ended up travelling for around 24 years."],
    [1, "What helped him find welcome and work so far from home?", ["Shared faith, law and a language of learning","Letters of introduction from European kings","Membership of a guild of professional explorers","Fluency in the languages of every region"], 0, "The wider Muslim world was a network that welcomed trained scholars with gifts, lodging and jobs."],
    [2, "What role did he hold at the Sultan's court in Delhi?", ["Army general","Judge","Treasurer","Court poet"], 1, "His legal training earned him a post as a qadi with a large salary."],
    [3, "Why do historians doubt parts of his account of China?", ["He never mentioned China in the book","Chinese records contradict all of it","It is vague and seems borrowed in places","He wrote it before leaving Morocco"], 2, "Vague passages and apparent borrowing mean not every claim in his account can be trusted."],
    [4, "Why do historians read the Rihla with care?", ["It was written in a secret code","Most copies were lost over time","It was written by people hostile to him","It was dictated from memory years later"], 3, "Memory, borrowing and exaggeration mean the Rihla is a rich but imperfect source."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"He left home at 21 for a pilgrimage and returned decades later. Where did he go?"});

WB.L("photography-invention", "explore", "beginner", "How photography was invented",
  "For thousands of years, people could watch an image of the world appear in a darkened room but had no way to keep it. Then chemistry caught up.",
  [
    ["Picture in a dark room", "The camera obscura projected images for centuries before anyone could capture one.",
      "Darken a room completely and make a small hole in one wall, and an upside-down image of the outside world appears on the opposite wall. This effect, the camera obscura (Latin for dark room), was described by thinkers in ancient China and Greece and studied carefully by the Arab scholar Ibn al-Haytham around 1,000 years ago. By the Renaissance, portable versions with lenses and mirrors helped artists trace scenes accurately.\n\nBut the image vanished the moment the light changed. Capturing it permanently needed a material that changed when light hit it, and a way to stop that change once the picture was made. Solving those two problems took until the nineteenth century."],
    ["The first photographs", "Niépce's hours-long exposure made the first surviving camera photograph, and Daguerre's 1839 process made sharp but unique images.",
      "In about 1826 or 1827, French inventor Nicéphore Niépce put a bitumen-coated pewter plate in a camera at a window of his country house. Bitumen hardens in light. After an exposure of many hours, possibly days, he washed away the unhardened parts, leaving a faint image of buildings and rooftops. View from the Window at Le Gras is the oldest surviving camera photograph. Because the sun moved during the exposure, buildings on both sides look lit.\n\nAfter Niépce died in 1833, his partner, the Parisian showman Louis Daguerre, developed a faster process. A silver-coated copper plate was sensitised with iodine fumes, exposed, then developed with toxic mercury vapour. The result was stunningly detailed, but each image was unique and could not be copied. In 1839 the French government acquired the rights and announced it to the world."],
    ["Negatives and copies", "Talbot's paper negatives let many prints be made from one image, the principle behind film photography.",
      "In England, William Henry Fox Talbot was working on his own method at the same time. He used paper soaked in silver salts, which darkened in light, to make negatives, images with light and dark reversed. By shining light through a negative onto another sheet of sensitive paper, he could make a positive print, again and again.\n\nHis improved process, the calotype, was patented in 1841. Calotypes were softer and less detailed than daguerreotypes, but the negative and positive principle was the future. Almost all film photography for the next 150 years followed Talbot's idea.\n\nOne of his earliest surviving negatives, from 1835, shows a window at his home, Lacock Abbey."],
    ["Photography spreads", "Faster processes took photography to wars and families, and George Eastman's Kodak made it simple for everyone.",
      "In 1851, Frederick Scott Archer introduced the wet collodion process: glass plates that gave fine detail and copies, with exposures of seconds. Photographers carried portable darkrooms to coat and develop each plate while wet. Photography recorded war, from the Crimean War to the American Civil War, and portrait studios let many families afford a likeness. An 1845 daguerreotype of Sydney doctor and politician William Bland is among Australia's earliest surviving photographs.\n\nPhotography was still fiddly until George Eastman launched the Kodak camera in 1888, sold loaded with film for about 100 pictures. Customers posted the camera back for developing and reloading. The slogan: \"You press the button, we do the rest.\" The 1900 Brownie box camera cost one dollar in the United States and put photography within reach of ordinary families."],
    ["Colour, then digital", "Colour film arrived in the twentieth century, and digital sensors then put a camera in every pocket.",
      "Early colour was difficult. The Lumière brothers released the Autochrome process in 1907, using dyed grains of potato starch to make dreamy colour images on glass. Easier colour film, such as Kodachrome, launched in 1935.\n\nThen came a bigger shift. In 1975 Kodak engineer Steven Sasson built an experimental digital camera. It was about the size of a toaster, captured black and white images of just 0.01 megapixels and recorded them onto cassette tape.\n\nKodak did not push the technology hard, partly because film was so profitable, and the company filed for bankruptcy protection in 2012. Today billions of people carry a camera in their phone, and well over a trillion photos are taken every year."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What problem did early camera obscura users face?", ["The image was always too blurry to use","The image vanished when the light changed","The image appeared only after dark","The image was too small to be seen"], 1, "Capturing the image needed a light-sensitive material and a way to stop the change."],
    [1, "Why do buildings on both sides of Niépce's first photograph look sunlit?", ["He used two cameras at once","The sun moved during a long exposure","He painted the shadows out afterwards","It was taken at midday in summer"], 1, "The very long exposure shows how slow the earliest process was."],
    [2, "What was the key idea in Talbot's process?", ["A positive print made directly on metal","Colour dyes in potato starch","A negative could make many positive prints","A glass plate kept wet"], 2, "The negative and positive principle allowed copies and shaped film photography for 150 years."],
    [3, "How did Eastman's Kodak make photography simple?", ["It printed instant pictures","It stored images on glass plates","It came loaded, and Kodak did the developing","It was made for professional studios"], 2, "\"You press the button, we do the rest\" removed the chemistry and expertise from the user."],
    [4, "Why did Kodak struggle after inventing the digital camera?", ["The sensor could not produce an image","It held back because film was so profitable","The company lost the patent to a rival","Customers had stopped wanting photographs"], 1, "Protecting a profitable film business delayed Kodak's move into digital."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The first photograph needed hours of exposure. Why does the sun appear on both sides?"});

WB.L("bicycle", "explore", "beginner", "The bicycle: a simple machine that changed society",
  "Two wheels, a frame and a chain. The bicycle looks so obvious it is hard to believe it took the best part of a century to get right, or that it helped change the lives of women.",
  [
    ["A running machine", "The first two-wheeler, built by Karl Drais in 1817, had no pedals and was pushed along with the feet.",
      "In 1817, German forestry official Karl Drais rode a strange new machine near Mannheim. It had two wheels in a line, a wooden frame and a seat, and he pushed it along by striding with his feet on the ground, much like a modern children's balance bike. He called it a Laufmaschine, or running machine. It became known as the draisine or dandy horse.\n\nSome historians link the invention to 1816, the Year Without a Summer, when a huge volcanic eruption in Indonesia had disrupted the climate. Crop failures made feeding horses expensive.\n\nThe dandy horse craze faded quickly, but the idea of balancing on two wheels had arrived."],
    ["Boneshakers and high wheelers", "Pedals fixed to the front wheel made bikes practical, and bigger front wheels made them faster but riskier.",
      "In the 1860s in France, makers attached pedals and cranks directly to the front wheel, so riders could lift their feet off the ground. Credit is disputed between the Michaux family business in Paris and a mechanic named Pierre Lallement. These velocipedes had iron frames and wooden wheels with iron rims, and on rough roads they rattled so badly they were nicknamed boneshakers. They were still popular, with riding schools and races.\n\nWith pedals fixed to the front wheel, each turn moved the bike only as far as its circumference. So makers enlarged the wheel. By the 1870s, high wheelers had front wheels well over a metre across, later nicknamed penny-farthings. Solid rubber tyres and wire spokes made them light and fast, but hitting a rock or pothole could pitch the rider head first over the handlebars."],
    ["The safety bicycle", "Chain drive, similar-sized wheels and air-filled tyres created the modern bicycle by the 1890s.",
      "The breakthrough came in 1885, when English inventor John Kemp Starley launched the Rover. It had two wheels of similar size and a chain driving the back wheel. Gearing in the chain drive meant a small wheel could still travel fast, and the rider sat low enough to put a foot down. It was called a safety bicycle, and its basic layout is still used today.\n\nComfort followed. In 1888, John Boyd Dunlop, a Scottish vet living in Belfast, developed an air-filled tyre for his young son's tricycle. Pneumatic tyres made riding far smoother and faster, and Dunlop's name became a famous brand.\n\nBy the 1890s, the modern bicycle was essentially complete."],
    ["Freedom on two wheels", "The 1890s bicycle boom gave many women independence and spread bikes into the Australian bush.",
      "The 1890s bicycle craze reached far beyond transport. For many women, the bicycle meant travelling without a chaperone or a horse and carriage. It also encouraged dress reform: long skirts and tight corsets were impractical, so some women adopted divided skirts or bloomers, to critics' outrage. In 1896 American campaigner Susan B. Anthony said the bicycle had done more to emancipate women than anything else in the world.\n\nAustralia caught the craze too. Cyclists carried mail across the Western Australian goldfields, shearers rode between stations, and some rode around the continent. Bikes were cheaper than horses and needed no feed or water, a big advantage in dry country. Hubert Opperman became a hero for endurance riding in the 1920s and 1930s, and in 1990 Victoria became the first place in the world to make bicycle helmets compulsory."],
    ["Small machine, big legacy", "Bicycle technology fed into cars and aeroplanes, and bikes remain among the most efficient ways to travel.",
      "The bicycle's influence spread into other machines. Makers developed and refined ball bearings, chain drives, pneumatic tyres and lightweight steel tubing, all of which fed into early cars and motorcycles. In some countries the campaign for better roads began with cyclists, before motorists took it over.\n\nMost famously, Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio, and used its skills, tools and profits to build the aeroplane they flew in 1903.\n\nToday the bicycle remains one of the most energy-efficient ways for a person to travel. In cities from Amsterdam to Copenhagen, bikes are used for a large share of daily trips, and cities around the world are building bike lanes to follow their lead."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How did riders move Karl Drais's 1817 machine?", ["By turning cranks by hand","With pedals on the back wheel","By striding along the ground","With a tiny steam engine"], 2, "It had no pedals: riders strode along, much like a modern balance bike."],
    [1, "Why did penny-farthings have such large front wheels?", ["To make room for a larger seat","So each pedal turn went further","To make them easier to mount","To carry more luggage"], 1, "With pedals fixed to the front wheel, a bigger wheel meant a longer distance per turn."],
    [2, "What made the 1885 safety bicycle safer than a high wheeler?", ["Hand brakes fitted to both wheels","A wider, heavier wooden frame","Similar-sized wheels and a chain drive","Solid rubber tyres on every wheel"], 2, "Chain drive let a small wheel go fast, so riders no longer perched high above the front wheel."],
    [3, "How did the bicycle change life for many women in the 1890s?", ["It was banned for women in most places","It replaced sewing as a hobby","It was mainly used for deliveries","It gave many women new independence"], 3, "Bikes let women travel without a chaperone and made impractical clothing hard to defend."],
    [4, "How did the bicycle help lead to the aeroplane?", ["Bicycle engines powered early aeroplanes","Their cycle shop gave them skills and funds","Aeroplane wings were made from bicycle frames","Pilots first had to qualify as cyclists"], 1, "The Wrights built their aeroplane using the skills, tools and profits of their bicycle business."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"It took nearly a century to get the bicycle right. What finally made it work?"});

